WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:10.750
The last pattern, or topic, under input controls is the date input. Date input covers more than one pattern, and

2
00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:19.000
we can use any of them depending on what exactly I need, to let the user enter the date they want.

3
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:28.675
If we look at the patterns under date input, the first is calendar pickers. We've almost all used a calendar picker: it shows me a calendar

4
00:00:28.675 --> 00:00:38.350
of a given month, and I choose the day from that month. Of course it suits picking a date close to today very well. And if I

5
00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:48.025
add a dropdown, or any other pattern or component that lets me change the year easily, it can suit even going back years or forward years,

6
00:00:48.025 --> 00:00:57.700
so it's easy to pick something in the year range I need. Calendar pickers are among the most frequently used patterns, and we've probably all used them.

7
00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:08.400
They're also very well suited to choosing a date range. If I want to choose a specific range, the best thing to use is pretty much a calendar picker. I can show two months, like this example,

8
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:19.100
as on most sites that need the user to enter a range of dates: two months appear side by side like this, so if they choose a date or range from the end of the first month into

9
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:29.800
the second, say the start of the second month, both show in front of them. The range is marked on the calendar, so they see it clearly. It's one of the best patterns for this case in particular.

10
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:41.560
The second pattern in date inputs is scrolling date pickers: I scroll until I reach the date I want, there may be several columns side by side, and they can include a time

11
00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:53.320
too if I want to enter one. Of course I won't use it, especially if I want to go to a far date, long ago or far ahead. I can use it to

12
00:01:53.320 --> 00:02:05.080
pick a near date, or to control the time: the time is all 24 hours, so I control the hours and minutes. But I won't go back months with a scrolling date picker;

13
00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:16.840
it won't be the most suitable, since I really move through a small range of dates. And if I'm choosing more than one date, a scrolling date picker may not be the best

14
00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:28.600
choice: the space can be small, and it won't be productive or help me choose quickly. I can let users simply type the date, and typing can be faster and better in that case.

15
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:37.900
The next pattern under date inputs is using dropdowns to choose the year, day and month. This pattern has a problem: it raises the interaction cost. I open the

16
00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:47.200
dropdown, maybe scroll inside it and choose the month, open another dropdown, scroll and choose the day, open and choose the year, and so on. So I have a

17
00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:56.500
bit more interaction cost to choose a single date, and it's generally never one of the preferred patterns for choosing a date. The patterns we covered before can be better.

18
00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:05.947
The last pattern is typing: I type the date. As long as I show the user where to type the day, where the month goes and where the year goes. I can move the user straight to the month as

19
00:03:05.947 --> 00:03:15.393
soon as they type the day, then to the year, or they type the month, it moves them to the day and then the year, and so on. When they type the month, for example, the slash appears and takes them

20
00:03:15.393 --> 00:03:24.840
to the day, and when they type the day it takes them to the year, so they understand exactly where they're typing. Then typing is one of the easiest kinds of input we can use to let someone enter a date.
